//
// `field_value` inside a generic body must be REFUSED, not answered emptily.
//
// A generic body is parsed once against its type variable, so there is no
// concrete type to read positions out of — the same limit `type_of` carries.
// The reading therefore found no field at any position and answered
// `OtherKind` for every one of them, which for the caller this API exists to
// serve (an ORM's write half) is an empty row rather than an error: the write
// succeeds, the columns are missing, and nothing says so.
//
// That is the same silent under-delivery as the `#fields` nullable drop
// (`pln23-field-iter-nullable.loft`), reached from the other side, so it gets
// the same treatment — say it where the author can see it, at compile time.
//
// The message names the type variable and what to do instead, because "not
// supported here" would leave the reader to discover that calling it one frame
// out works.

// @EXPECT_ERROR: field_value cannot read a value of the type variable `T`
struct P { id: integer, nm: text }

fn walk <T> (v: T) -> integer {
  fv = field_value(v, 0);
  return fv.i;
}

fn main() {
  p = P { id: 4242, nm: "ada" };
  println("{walk(p)}");
}
